<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- NuGet packaging metadata -->
    <Description>Core building blocks for the SharedKernel: base exception hierarchy, Result&lt;T&gt; railway extension methods (Map, Bind, Match, Tap, async), BCL extension methods (string, IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;, DateTimeOffset, Guid), and the two-path guard clause system (Guard.Against.* functional, Guard.Throw.* imperative — merged from SharedKernel.Guards, P-505/WO-082, same SharedKernel.Guards namespace preserved unchanged). Zero third-party NuGet dependencies. Depends on SharedKernel.Primitives.</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>WO-082 / P-505 (BREAKING — package retirement, no namespace/source change for consumers):
- SharedKernel.Guards.csproj no longer exists as a separately packed/published project. Every type it contained (Guard, Guard.Against.*, Guard.Throw.*, IGuardClause, GuardDescriptions) now lives inside this package, under the exact same SharedKernel.Guards / SharedKernel.Guards.Clauses / SharedKernel.Guards.Descriptions namespaces. A consumer's only required change is swapping the PackageReference from SharedKernel.Guards to SharedKernel.Core — no source edit.
WO-083 / P-522 (additive, non-breaking):
- The compiled-Regex cache backing Guard.Against.InvalidFormat/.Email is now bounded (a fixed maximum distinct-pattern count with oldest-first eviction) instead of growing without bound on every distinct caller-supplied pattern ever seen. Every existing call site passes a literal, compile-time-known pattern, so eviction never triggers in practice — this closes a hypothetical future vector where a pattern derived from configuration or user input could grow the cache and its JIT-compilation cost without limit.
WO-083 / P-510 (BREAKING BEHAVIOR CHANGE — no signature changes):
- ResultTry.Try/TryAsync's default (no custom onException) exception mapping no longer interpolates the caught exception's raw "{ExceptionType}: {ExceptionMessage}" into Error.Message. Error.Message is now always the fixed, safe ResultTry.DefaultUnexpectedMessage string. A caught exception (e.g. a database driver or third-party SDK error) can carry sensitive text — connection-string fragments, usernames, internal hostnames — that must never reach an HTTP response via Error.ToProblemDetails(). The raw exception detail is now recorded on the ambient System.Diagnostics.Activity via Activity.Current?.AddException(exception) (a .NET 8+ BCL member, zero new dependency) instead — one call per flattened AggregateException inner exception. A caller supplying its own onException mapper is completely unaffected; it still receives the raw exception exactly as before.
- Every ResultTry catch clause (Try, Try w/ mapper, TryAsync, TryAsync w/ mapper) now excludes OperationCanceledException (and its subclass TaskCanceledException) via `catch (Exception exception) when (exception is not OperationCanceledException)`. A genuine cancellation now always propagates as a thrown exception instead of being silently converted into a Result.Failure — a client disconnect must not become a synthesized failure result that looks like an ordinary error.
- Honors this same security audit's ratified DECLINED "no metadata bag on Error" ruling: Error stays exactly (Code, Message, Type) — no new field was added to carry exception detail.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <PackageTags>shared-kernel;railway-oriented;exceptions;extensions;guard;guard-clause;validation;aot</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SharedKernel.Primitives\SharedKernel.Primitives.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
